    // The fuzzer will supply oracleVersionId. The general rule: for any id, c.atVersion(id)
    // must return an OracleVersion whose price equals the transformed price. We cannot call
    // internal _transform, but we know C.__UPayoffProvider__initialize applies payoffDefinition.transform
    // via _transform when returning. To make transform a no-op in reference, we rely on payoffDefinition
    // default behavior in the deployed C (payoffDefinition used in initialize). However we cannot call
    // initialize on C because it expects complex ProductInfo. Instead, we only call the public atVersion
    // which in reference delegates to oracle().atVersion and then _transform in view; to exercise that,
    // we first set the stored oracle address by calling updateOracle (needs onlyProductOwner) is restricted.
    // Instead, we exploit that C.atVersion calls oracle().atVersion; we compare c.atVersion(id) with a
    // local transformation of oracle().atVersion(id) by reading oracle directly and applying the same
    // transform the product would (we model transform as identity here because we cannot access payoffDefinition).
    // To guarantee the property distinguishes the mutant, we assert that calling c.atVersion(id) returns a price
    // that differs from the raw oracle.atVersion(id).price for some ids — i.e., the reference applies transform,
